WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.680
In my last video, I talked about
energy and the importance of energy.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.520
Today, I want to talk about relaxing.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:11.739
Some people commented that they have
trouble generating enough energy.

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:13.859
They very quickly feel tired.

00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:16.219
And so they aren't able to continue
with their language learning.

00:00:16.319 --> 00:00:19.009
So I think it's important to
recognize that we are all different.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:22.800
And I have my experience, I like
learning languages, I've learned a lot

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.680
to varying degrees, and I share with
you my experience, but I fully recognize

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.270
that other people have, uh, different
makeups and have different interests.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.720
However, I will continue to
share with you my thoughts.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.550
I think it's important that we
vary intensity and relaxation.

00:00:38.699 --> 00:00:39.970
We need both.

00:00:40.235 --> 00:00:42.224
We very much need intensity.

00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:45.214
I have found that if I'm going
at a language, I want to have

00:00:45.214 --> 00:00:46.684
a certain amount of intensity.

00:00:46.904 --> 00:00:47.815
This will vary.

00:00:48.044 --> 00:00:52.674
When I was learning Chinese
back in 1968, that was my job.

00:00:52.855 --> 00:00:56.214
As a Canadian diplomat, I
was paid to learn Chinese.

00:00:56.434 --> 00:00:59.224
So I went at it full
time, three hours a day.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:05.920
And I would practice, you know, learning
characters, or I would listen to things

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.590
on my big open reel tape recorder, and I
was at it five, six hours a day, intense.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:15.720
And yet still I would vary my activities
and I would also find time to be with

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.600
friends, find time to play tennis.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.490
I was never running out of gas.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.430
I was never burned out from my
efforts at learning Chinese, but I

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.470
was going at it quite intensively.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:29.110
That's the most intensely I
have ever studied a language.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:34.460
Now I learn languages for fun, so
I might go an hour, an hour or so a

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:39.240
day on my iPad using LingQ, looking
at words or listening to things.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.389
But the important thing is, in my
view, when I find that say I've

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:48.280
been doing something on the iPad
and I feel, you know, I'm tired now.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.150
I've been doing this for half an hour,
for an hour, for an hour and a half.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:51.900
Stop.

00:01:52.115 --> 00:01:52.435
Okay.

00:01:52.885 --> 00:01:55.875
And it's perfectly legitimate to stop.

00:01:56.155 --> 00:01:59.505
You shouldn't push yourself beyond
the point where you're starting

00:01:59.505 --> 00:02:01.155
to feel that it's a chore.

00:02:01.274 --> 00:02:02.474
It shouldn't be a chore.

00:02:02.504 --> 00:02:06.554
And what makes it not a chore is if
you do activities that you enjoy doing

00:02:06.584 --> 00:02:08.455
and that you vary these activities.

00:02:08.465 --> 00:02:10.044
So right now in Arabic.

00:02:10.395 --> 00:02:13.815
I'm listening to, uh, interviews
in Arabic in particular.

00:02:13.815 --> 00:02:18.835
I'm going through an interview with Carlos
Rosen, who is the Lebanese, Brazilian,

00:02:18.845 --> 00:02:23.495
French president of Nissan in Japan,
amongst other things in his career.

00:02:23.785 --> 00:02:25.234
And it's a very interesting story.

00:02:25.234 --> 00:02:28.265
And he's a fascinating person
to listen to in four languages.

00:02:28.265 --> 00:02:31.565
And of course, on YouTube, I can find
his interviews in different languages

00:02:31.585 --> 00:02:36.045
and the Arabic ones, I bring them in and
I study them on LingQ, but I do them.

00:02:36.085 --> 00:02:36.805
I enjoy them.

00:02:36.904 --> 00:02:39.765
And to me, the important
thing is that I am active.

00:02:40.855 --> 00:02:44.755
Another aspect of relaxation,
and I liken sort of language

00:02:44.755 --> 00:02:46.565
learning to long distance running.

00:02:46.855 --> 00:02:51.065
If I'm going to go for a jog for
half an hour Uh, through the forest,

00:02:51.175 --> 00:02:54.835
uh, I'm not going to sprint because
I've got to make it all the way back.

00:02:54.895 --> 00:02:57.655
And I intend to enjoy the
run through the forest.

00:02:57.715 --> 00:03:00.365
That's not to say that it
may not be a good thing at

00:03:00.374 --> 00:03:01.724
times to sprint a little bit.

00:03:01.735 --> 00:03:05.154
You know, you have this fart leg
run, uh, little bit of aerobic

00:03:05.155 --> 00:03:08.805
and then anaerobic activity, but
basically I have to pace myself.

00:03:09.505 --> 00:03:13.795
Another thing about pacing
yourself is that you have to have.

00:03:13.925 --> 00:03:15.675
Good sleeping habits.

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:18.375
Again, easily said, there are some
people who have trouble sleeping.

00:03:18.415 --> 00:03:18.935
I don't know.

00:03:18.935 --> 00:03:22.374
I certainly never sleep through
the night, but, uh, sleep is

00:03:22.374 --> 00:03:25.535
where we kind of consolidate the
things that we've been learning.

00:03:25.574 --> 00:03:28.055
So I sometimes hear people say,
well, you know, I'm going to

00:03:28.055 --> 00:03:32.235
put my Spanish or Chinese on
and fall asleep listening to it.

00:03:32.264 --> 00:03:34.465
And that way I'll learn it while sleeping.

00:03:34.495 --> 00:03:35.805
All you do is ruin your sleep.

00:03:36.155 --> 00:03:41.765
I will leave a link to an article
from a website called News in Health.

00:03:42.019 --> 00:03:46.359
And it explains just how important sleep
is to consolidate what you have learned,

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:50.129
to consolidate the memories, how the
brain continues to hang on to the things

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:51.829
that you have learned during the day.

00:03:51.829 --> 00:03:54.119
So, sleep is important.

00:03:54.579 --> 00:03:58.299
Another thing that I think is relevant to
this discussion of, you know, intensity

00:03:58.299 --> 00:04:02.949
and then relaxation, is that I have
always found that if I leave a language.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:08.030
For a while, and particularly if I
study another language, then when

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:11.910
I come back to the first language,
I'm often better than I was.

00:04:11.939 --> 00:04:16.720
I've called this sort of benign neglect,
and I'm not sure whether the language

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:19.449
continues to gestate in your brain.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:22.909
Or whether it's because you
tried this other language and

00:04:22.909 --> 00:04:24.409
struggled with that for a while.

00:04:24.409 --> 00:04:27.330
Then you come back to the first
one and the first one seems easier.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:30.789
But I have always found that,
that there is no loss in

00:04:30.799 --> 00:04:32.400
leaving a language for a while.

00:04:32.569 --> 00:04:35.830
Other than the fact that you're not
spending that time with the language.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:39.947
So you will So maybe you should, you
know, perhaps progress more slowly by not

00:04:39.947 --> 00:04:41.778
having spent the time with the language.

00:04:41.778 --> 00:04:43.510
However, you won't slip very much.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:48.309
And so I often hear people say that
they're afraid to stop working on

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.979
this language because they're actually
interested in this other language and

00:04:51.979 --> 00:04:53.190
then they'll lose the first language.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:55.540
I've never found that to be the case.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:05:00.700
I think we can vary intensity with periods
of benign neglect where we don't do

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.909
anything and we don't suffer that much.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:06.859
And if we come back refreshed,
then so much the better.

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:08.570
And now it's important to remember.

00:05:08.785 --> 00:05:13.445
That when we're studying, we shouldn't be
too fussed, and I mentioned this in the

00:05:13.445 --> 00:05:17.424
last video about energy, we shouldn't be
too fussed about how much we're learning.

00:05:17.595 --> 00:05:20.945
Like, I've gotta learn this, and I have
to go through all these flashcards,

00:05:20.945 --> 00:05:22.015
and I have to do this and that.

00:05:22.505 --> 00:05:24.995
All of that pressure is
sort of counterproductive.

00:05:25.055 --> 00:05:29.385
You have to have the confidence and
the trust that your brain is learning.

00:05:29.775 --> 00:05:32.940
That as long as you are
active, That you will learn.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.010
People sometimes ask me, Oh, Steve,
you have this number, so many

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:38.150
known words, 20, 000 known words.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:39.709
Do you really know those words?

00:05:39.709 --> 00:05:41.020
Can you produce those words?

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.119
And I say, well, probably not.

00:05:43.379 --> 00:05:46.859
However, it reflects the fact that
when I see that word on a text

00:05:46.869 --> 00:05:50.340
in LingQ, I don't save it because
I understand it in that context.

00:05:50.419 --> 00:05:53.369
Eventually, I will be able
to use it, but no guarantees.

00:05:53.559 --> 00:05:58.719
However, those statistics, known words,
number of LingQs created on LingQ,

00:05:58.809 --> 00:06:02.770
hours of listening even, All of those
are measures of your activity because

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:07.880
the most important thing is that you
stay with the task and remain active.

00:06:08.099 --> 00:06:09.700
Don't put pressure on yourself.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:12.620
Don't force yourself to do
activities that you don't like to do.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:17.219
Don't do things that are likely
to make you run out of energy.

00:06:17.229 --> 00:06:18.469
Vary the activities.

00:06:18.794 --> 00:06:23.224
Very studying hard with relaxation
by all means, find someone, if you

00:06:23.224 --> 00:06:26.944
can find someone to talk to in the
language and that if it's a low

00:06:26.944 --> 00:06:28.635
stress environment, that's all good.

00:06:28.664 --> 00:06:31.624
So I think we don't all have
the same level of energy.

00:06:31.654 --> 00:06:34.294
We should all be mixing
in periods of relaxation.

00:06:34.465 --> 00:06:38.414
We shouldn't be afraid either to
relax or to study another language.

00:06:38.455 --> 00:06:41.515
We should apply the level of
energy that we have and do things

00:06:41.825 --> 00:06:43.795
that either require less energy.

00:06:43.945 --> 00:06:45.735
Or, give us a sense of energy.

00:06:45.735 --> 00:06:48.895
And part of that is to give yourself
credit for what you're doing.

00:06:48.965 --> 00:06:53.035
And mainly that means give yourself
credit for being active, remaining

00:06:53.035 --> 00:06:54.825
active in learning the language.

00:06:55.015 --> 00:06:55.854
Thank you for listening.

00:06:55.975 --> 00:06:56.394
Bye for now.

